  "query_preview": "..Remnants of a weakening storm system continues to\nlift across Arizona into the Four Corners area today. With\nlingering moisture in place, weak forcing and very modest\ninstability should be enough to maintain some generally weak \nshowers through the rest of the day across mainly the higher\nterrain of southern and central Utah (with possibly a couple of \nthunderstorms in southwest Utah). \n\nTomorrow into Wednesday, expect southwesterly flow to \nprogressively increase over Utah as a closed low slowly slides \ndown the California coast. This southwesterly flow will maintain \ntemperatures generally above climatological normals across the \narea. As instability increases tomorrow along with some \ndeveloping upper diffluence, expect a slight increase in shower \ncoverage and thunderstorm potential, focused primarily over the \nspine of Utah and western Utah. \n\nBy Wednesday afternoon, a jet nosing into southern Utah will be \naccompanied by a plume of moisture with origins near what is \ncurrently Tropical Storm Trudy. The bulk of this moisture looks to\ncross the southeastern portions of the forecast area. \nAdditionally, the latest deterministic models are now a bit faster\nwith the onset of precipitation on Wednesday compared to other \nrecent runs. As such, expect precipitation to increase from the\nsouth on Wednesday, then become more widespread across the\nforecast area on Thursday as the storm system moves inland.\nLocally moderate to heavy precipitation can be expected, with a\nthreat of flash flooding especially in prone areas of southern \nand central Utah. There is currently a Slight Risk of excessive\nrainfall for southern Utah Wednesday and Thursday. The NBM \nindicates a broad area of 1 to 2.5 inches of QPF extending from\nfar southwest Utah through southeast and east-central Utah.\nElsewhere, the NBM has up to 1 inch except around 1.5 inches in\nthe Uintas. In addition to the heavy rain, thunderstorms could \ntrend on the stronger side across southern Utah on Wednesday and \nboth southern and northern Utah on Thursday owing to increased \ninstability, shear, and large-scale forcing. \n\nOn Friday, the heavier precipitation will become more confined to\nnorthern Utah. With cooler air arriving late Thursday night into \nFriday, snow levels will lower, bringing accumulating snow to the \nmountains. Latest deterministic GFS and EC bring H7 temperatures \ndown to -3 to -4C over northern Utah. Snow levels could \npotentially briefly reach 6.5kft, though most accumulations will \nlikely remain above 8kft. Around 3-6 inches with locally up to 9\ninches in the upper Cottonwoods and high Uintas are currently\nforecast. \n\nBeyond Friday, there continues to be some model spread with the\nlarge-scale pattern. In general, however, there looks to be a\ndrying and warming trend over Utah continues through early next\nweek.",
